sec.p:
Hops-to-root-cause is computed for both trace forms. Table 2 reports the mean and 90th-percentile hops-to-root-cause per incident class. Mean hops-to-root-cause falls from 6.4 under flat-span analysis to 1.5 under causal-link traversal, a factor of 4.3 across the 20-incident corpus. The largest reductions occur in the rate-limit (4.7

    ×

) and large language model hallucination (4.5

    ×

) classes. All incident classes share the same four pipeline depths ({2, 3, 5, 8}) by design; the larger reductions in these two classes reflect the fact that their fault patterns tend to manifest later in the execution trace, increasing the flat-span hop count while the causal-link distance remains bounded by directed acyclic graph depth. The smallest reduction is in tool-argument malformation (3.1

    ×

) because that class has shallow trajectories. Recall is exact in both forms---the ground-truth causal span is found in every incident. The metric measures investigation efficiency, not correctness. Table 3 reports the causal-link instrumentation impact by pipeline depth.
